WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:07.624
If you dream of owning a big house and a nice car,
you study hard and slowly climb the ladder,

00:00:07.624 --> 00:00:09.470
with pure effort and determination.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.618
But you need not work so hard.

00:00:11.618 --> 00:00:15.229
There's an easier way to make
much more money, much much faster.

00:00:15.539 --> 00:00:20.509
The door to your success is in making 
a mobile game in the keys we will soon discover.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.140
But you must be thinking
"Isn't the market already pretty flooded?"

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:29.120
The answer is "Yes, incredibly!"
But nearly all your competition has failed miserably.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.599
Too generous. Too original. Too honest.

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:36.769
There's always more space for those
that carefully follow the lessons to come.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.122
Just look at the hundreds of
best-selling slot machine apps:

00:00:40.122 --> 00:00:43.459
each is just a slightly different
face to the same basic game,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.060
but they're all incredibly successful.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.420
Candy crush makes not 1, or 5,
or 50 million dollars a year,

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.520
but over a billion.
They're doing something very right.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:59.700
Either Candy Crush is the perfect
realization of the art of game design,

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.480
or it's really good at exploiting your brain.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.900
So what tricks of psychology
can we learn from the very best?

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.960
You will soon find that they are no
more difficult than taking candy from a child.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:21.460
If you've ever played these games, you may have
noticed that they all have their own currency.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.180
Instead of paying real money to save your
character's life or change his appearance,

00:01:25.539 --> 00:01:30.009
you pay with gems, or coins, or stars,
which you first buy with real money.

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:31.660
This is no accident.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:35.700
By abstracting real money into made-up
currency, players are distanced from

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.280
the consequences of their choices.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.920
I'm not paying $2 for this virtual hat,
I'm paying 500 coins,

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.340
which happened to cost two dollars.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.489
If you don't think anyone could
possibly be that stupid, think again.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.814
We all do this to some extent when
we see a $10 item as simply costing one of these

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:57.480
and not what we're actually paying with,
like, say, an hour of minimum wage work.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:02.120
Casinos use this trick all the time.
Paying with tokens feels different than

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.160
paying with the same amount in dollars.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.540
And, instead of constantly asking
the player to buy this or that with real money,

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.500
tokens make the occasional moment of weakness
enough to make a handsome profit.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:17.040
So, instead of selling one life
for a dollar, you can sell packs of coins,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.740
the cheapest of which is $2 or $5 or $10.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:25.520
The player is forced to buy a handful of coins
when they only need a few for what they really want.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.800
The smartest games multiply this effect
to create even more abstractions.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.420
In Clash of Clans, you can buy gems,
which can be used to buy gold,

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.460
which you can then use to buy items.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.170
The more indirect, the more you collect.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:48.075
Every game has two kinds of players:
Those that play for free and a tiny fraction

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.200
that buys enough to make up for everybody else.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.129
Your goal is to create as many
of that second category as possible.

00:02:55.129 --> 00:02:58.780
You want them to be dependent
on your game for satisfaction,

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.420
and to do that you need to create routine.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.580
The player who pays a lot is the one who plays a lot.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:08.360
In the morning, on the commute,
at the dinner table... everywhere.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:13.660
Reward players for opening your app every day,
and create a streak system that gives prizes

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.720
for playing X amount of days in a row.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.380
It doesn't take long before this creates a regular habit.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.478
Some would say, an addiction. And when it does,
players can justify paying even more.

00:03:23.478 --> 00:03:26.860
"Sure, I'm spending a ton, but I play this game a lot."

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.940
And don't let anyone escape your grasp.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:35.620
If you notice a player has taken a break,
lure them back with a notification, or two, or thirty.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.350
People will happily pay for 
more levels spins and characters,

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.700
but your ultimate goal is to monetize everything.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.520
If you can make your players pay to play,
you can also make them pay not to play.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.540
If you intentionally create annoyances
and timesinks, parts of your game

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.300
that are no fun, but necessary, 
players will pay to skip them.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.900
It may sound ridiculous that someone would pay
money to play less of the game they've paid for,

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:11.200
but it works. To your players, it will feel like
any other purchase, and you will make money

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.020
even when your game is boring.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.820
Win-win.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:25.180
If a casino has $1000 for prize money,
It would usually rather give a hundred $10 prizes

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:26.960
than a single thousand dollar prize.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:32.120
Too much happiness at once leaves
a player satisfied, more likely to cash out and leave.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.880
So too with your game. Your goal
is not to make your players happy.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:40.280
You'll often actually want to do the opposite,
so space out the enjoyment of your game,

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.560
enough to keep people interested,
but never enough to truly satisfy.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.560
And there shouldn't be a single goal,
but a series of objectives,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.700
always replaced with another
when you complete the last.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:56.160
The most successful games aren't stories,
with an arc and a gratifying ending,

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.100
but eternal tasks with fake accomplishments.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:05.480
To take this a step further, create many similar games,
so there's always another app for people to download.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:12.029
A good developer makes a single game, which they then
change just enough to sell again, and again, and again.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:19.480
While the tone of this video is sarcastic, these are all
real tactics used by the most popular games.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:23.640
By understanding what they're doing,
you're now less susceptible to their deception.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.820
But there's also more to this story, because
it wasn't always this bad.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:32.879
When the App Store began, It was common to see
$5 or $10 games that sold quite well.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.760
So what changed in the nearly 10 years since then?
And is there a way to return to more honest software?

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.680
These are big questions we'll explore in an upcoming video, so stay tuned and thanks for watching.

